<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tupelo, Mississippi</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tupelo is a city in north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-river">Mississippi</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, the seat of Lee County. It lies in the heart of the old Chickasaw homeland, and the battles of Ackia and Ogoula Tchetoka between the Chickasaw and the French in 1736 were fought nearby. A battle of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-civil-war">American Civil War</a> took place there in 1864, and the city is known as the birthplace of Elvis Presley in 1935.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tupelo-mississippi.jpg" alt="Tupelo, Mississippi" width="320" height="156" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Fredlyfish4 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Downtown_Tupelo_1.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Tupelo lies in north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-river">Mississippi</a> on Interstate 22 and U.S. Route 78, midway between Memphis, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tennessee">Tennessee</a>, and Birmingham, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alabama">Alabama</a>. It covers about 51 square miles, and the 2020 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> counted 37,923 people. The Tupelo area is also the headquarters of the Natchez Trace Parkway, which follows the route of an ancient trail used by indigenous peoples long before Europeans came and connects Natchez, Mississippi, to Nashville, Tennessee. Nearby are the Pharr Mounds, a complex of nearly 2,000-year-old burial earthworks of the Middle Woodland period.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Chickasaw and Choctaw occupied the area before European settlement, and the French and British traded with them and sought alliances. On 26 May 1736 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-ackia">Battle of Ackia</a> was fought near the site of present-day Tupelo, where British and Chickasaw soldiers repelled a French and Choctaw attack on the Chickasaw village of Ackia. The French governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louisiana">Louisiana</a>, Jean Baptiste Le Moyne, Sieur de Bienville, had sought to link Louisiana with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acadia">Acadia</a> and the other northern colonies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-france">New France</a>. The Indian Removal Act of 1830 authorised the relocation of the Southeast Native Americans to territory west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-river">Mississippi River</a>, completed by the end of the 1830s.</p><p>The settlement was founded in 1860 on the Mobile and Ohio Railroad and was first called Gum Pond, from the local tupelo trees known as blackgum. Union and Confederate forces fought in the area in 1864 in the Battle of Tupelo and the Battle of Old Town Creek. The town took the name Tupelo in honour of the battle and was incorporated on 20 July 1870.</p><p>By the early twentieth century it had <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> textile mills, which under segregation employed only white adults and children. On 7 February 1934 Tupelo became the first city to receive power from the Tennessee Valley Authority, and President Franklin Roosevelt visited this First TVA City in 1935.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>On 5 and 6 April 1936 a tornado struck, levelling 48 city blocks and more than 200 homes and killing 216 people in the official count. It destroyed the Gum Pond neighbourhood. Because white newspapers did not report news about Black people until the 1940s and 1950s, historians estimate the real toll was higher. Elvis Presley, then a baby, survived it.</p><p>Tupelo is the seat of Lee County and the birthplace of Elvis Presley, and the Elvis Presley Birthplace is among its cultural institutions. The Tupelo National Battlefield and the Brices Cross Roads National Battlefield, about ten miles to the north, commemorate Civil War battles. A statue of the Chickasaw leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piomingo">Piomingo</a> stands in front of the city hall.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tupelo, Mississippi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>By around 1700 they lived in a cluster of towns near modern <b>Tupelo</b> and had become key partners of English traders from Carolina.</blockquote><a href="/state/chickasaw-nation">Chickasaw Nation</a></li></ul></section>
